WEBVTT

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.869
Start FANTASY with the command, fantasy app. Choose Single spectrum.

00:00:05.219 --> 00:00:13.481
Under Files, choose the bundled SDSS spectrum ending in zero four five three. Check the filename and redshift in the loaded spectrum table.

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:21.513
Open Prepare. Use the redshift and instrumental resolution from the FITS file, and keep Move to rest frame selected.

00:00:21.863 --> 00:00:30.247
For this offline example, choose None under Galactic extinction. The flux you measure will include the effect of foreground dust. Then choose the model.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:35.068
Select the Hβ preset and keep two broad components.

00:00:35.418 --> 00:00:42.939
In the component list, find the continuum, Fe II emission, and line groups. The initial guess shows how they will be fitted together.

00:00:44.128 --> 00:00:48.190
Choose Set up fit and keep the covariance error estimate for this first pass.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:52.242
Click Run fit. When the fit finishes, choose See the results.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:01:03.719
The summary gives the width of the combined broad Hβ profile. Here it is 4,552 km/s, with a fit uncertainty of 69 km/s.

00:01:04.069 --> 00:01:09.421
The next tile gives the integrated broad-line flux. Read it with the units shown beneath the value.

00:01:10.628 --> 00:01:16.898
The yellow warning says a parameter ended at its limit. Inspect that warning even when the reduced chi squared looks good.

00:01:17.248 --> 00:01:25.566
Open Checks, expand Does the model miss anything, and choose Analyse residuals. Use the flagged wavelengths as starting points for inspection.

00:01:26.753 --> 00:01:31.333
Open Download and choose Fitted lines to save the line measurements as a CSV table.

00:01:31.683 --> 00:01:39.995
To repeat the analysis, open the three-dot menu at the top and choose Download script. The Python file contains the steps you ran in this session.
